From: =?UTF-8?q?J=C3=A1nos=20Mik=C3=B3?=
 <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 23:44:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 029/154] atrium: skip unrealized windows in GetBrowserWindows
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Clicking the Dock icon terminated the app:

  NSInvalidArgumentException, '*** -[__NSSetM addObject:]: object cannot be nil'
    -[__NSSetM addObject:] <- GetBrowserWindows <- BrowserCollection::ForEach
    <- applicationShouldHandleReopen:hasVisibleWindows:

GetBrowserWindows inserted

  browser_window_interface->GetWindow()->GetNativeWindow().GetNativeNSWindow()

with nothing checked along the way. A browser whose native window is not
realized yields nil, and NSMutableSet throws on it.

The invariant is the one patch 0018 already wrote down for the presenter
table: a collection entry does not imply a showable window. Closing a window
destroys its NSWindow immediately while the owning teardown runs later, so in
that interval the collection still names a browser whose window is gone. The
crash trace has no Atrium frames because the defect is in this upstream
helper; what Atrium changes is how often a browser exists without a realized
window.

Skipped rather than surfaced, matching patch 0018: an unrealized window is
not user-facing, so leaving it out of the focus set is the right answer. The
log names WHICH entry produced the nil, including whether GetWindow() itself
was null, because a guard that only reports "one was nil" leaves the next
occurrence as hard to diagnose as this one.

Verified by build/scenarios/dock-reopen-window-counts.json, 3 passed /
0 failed: four real Dock-reopen Apple Events across one and two windows,
with liveness asserted before the quit. Not claimed: the original teardown
race is not reproduced, and a healthy run has no nil to skip, so the run
proves no crash rather than proving the guard fired.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 chrome/browser/app_controller_mac.mm | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/app_controller_mac.mm b/chrome/browser/app_controller_mac.mm
index 15662c668c8a3..90313ec7137c9 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/app_controller_mac.mm
+++ b/chrome/browser/app_controller_mac.mm
@@ -346,9 +346,31 @@ NSSet<NSWindow*>* GetBrowserWindows() {
             BrowserWindowInterface::TYPE_APP) {
           return true;
         }
-        [result addObject:browser_window_interface->GetWindow()
-                              ->GetNativeWindow()
-                              .GetNativeNSWindow()];
+        // Crash guard: a collection entry does not imply a realized
+        // window. Closing a window destroys its NSWindow immediately while
+        // the owning teardown runs later, so in that interval the collection
+        // still names a browser whose native window is already gone. Adding
+        // that nil to an NSMutableSet throws NSInvalidArgumentException and
+        // terminates the app, which is what a Dock click during teardown did.
+        // Same invariant patch 0018 documents for the presenter table.
+        //
+        // Skipped rather than surfaced: an unrealized window is not
+        // user-facing, so leaving it out of the focus set is the correct
+        // answer. The log names WHICH entry produced the nil, because a
+        // guard that only says "one was nil" leaves the next occurrence
+        // exactly as hard to diagnose as this one was.
+        ui::BaseWindow* const window = browser_window_interface->GetWindow();
+        NSWindow* const native_window =
+            window ? window->GetNativeWindow().GetNativeNSWindow() : nil;
+        if (!native_window) {
+          VLOG(1) << "Atrium: skipping browser with no realized window in "
+                     "GetBrowserWindows (type "
+                  << browser_window_interface->GetType()
+                  << ", BaseWindow " << (window ? "present" : "null")
+                  << ") - teardown in flight";
+          return true;
+        }
+        [result addObject:native_window];
         return true;
       });
   return result;
